/usr/bin/compress from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for compress, so this is the disassembly, and compress.c is
that disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f compress.ctl compress.dist

compress.dist is the binary off inode 60 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 5429
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header - see
../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 1341 and c.ents
at 11f4.

Its own code is main at 0134, putlit at 034a and the message routine at 038a;
the rest is the library and is the same as expand's: finit at 03f5, the stream
routines getc 0569, putc 0603 and write 06d9, the string writer 0eae and
create 0f4f.

THE FORMAT is the thing this program is for, and expand reads it back.

    n  b1 ... bn     a literal block: n is 1 to 254
    0377  b  n       a run block: 0377, the byte, a count of 5 to 254

The loop at 0245 reads a byte c and then reads on while the next byte is the
same and the count is under 254.  A run of five or more becomes a run block;
anything shorter is put in a 254 byte holding buffer, which is sent when it
fills or when a run block has to go ahead of what comes next.  A run of more
than 254 comes out as a series of blocks, because the byte that ended the
first is the first of the next.  A literal count is therefore never 255, and
0377 is never ambiguous.  It is the whole of the compression: "most text files
are probably not worth compressing", as the manual says, and seven per cent
on a binary is its own estimate.  The compress of compress.dist is 4800 bytes
from 5429.

The options are the same as expand's - none, one or two names - and so are
the mistakes: the usage line, or the name and ": can't open." on the standard
error and an exit of status 0.  The output is created 0777.

The original's one-name form does not work.  The output stream is attached to
its file descriptor only when there are two names, so with one the first full
buffer jumps through a zero pointer - the sim reports a stack overflow at pc 0.
compress.c does what the manual says: one name in, the standard output out.

The exit status of the original is whatever was in BC at the end of main for
a good run - which was the return of the flush, a small garbage number - and 0
for a mistake.  This exits 0 and 1.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, on a file of long runs
and short runs and noise (98539 bytes), random bytes, an empty file, small
cases around five, and the binary itself; then through the original expand and
this expand both ways.  The messages and the status of every mistake match.
